/usr/bin/common from the Micronix 1.6 distribution

There is no source for common, so this is the disassembly, and common.c is that
disassembly read back into C.

    disas -f common.ctl common.dist

common.dist is the binary off inode 62 of disks/dist/1012-8_dist_2.IMD, 7125
bytes, mode 0711, dated 7 August 1982.  It has no header - see
../newuser/README - and uses Whitesmith's runtime, c.ent at 17d1 and c.ents at
1640.  It is the program of that name in Kernighan and Plauger's Software Tools:
what comm does, with the columns in a different order and the lines' leading
blanks taken off.

Its own code is the first dozen functions, to 05f8, and the library follows.
The data it keeps are words, and they matter: the last comparison (1c0d, which
starts at 0), the width (1c0f, 80), the width of a column (1c11, 26), the three
column flags (1803, 1805 and 1807), the two file names (1809 and 180b) and the
two line buffers of 512 bytes (180d and 1a0d).

main calls options (02d9) and then merge (012c) until it is done.  options goes
over the arguments: "-1", "-2" and "-3" turn on a column, a word that begins
"-w" is the width, and any other word is a file name, the first for file1 and
the second for file2 and the rest ignored.  The test that nothing was asked for
- and so all three columns are wanted - is made after every argument, a file
name among them; and so is the division of the width by the number of columns.
It is the usage line, "usage: common [-1] [-2] [-3] [-wN] file1 [file2]", on the
standard error if there is no file1 - and it does not stop there, but goes on
to read the standard input as the first file, and as the second.  The second
file is the standard input anyway if it is not named.

merge reads a line into line1 if the last comparison was not positive and into
line2 if it was not negative, so the first pass reads both, with getl (07fc,
handed 512: up to a newline or 512 characters, newline included, and a count,
the end of its file being 0).  A line is ended with a NUL.  If both are empty it
is done.  compare (019f) decides: an empty first line is greater, an empty
second is less, and otherwise the first byte that is different, as signed
characters, newline included.  output (0236) writes by that: less - the line is
only in the first file - is column 1 if it is wanted; greater is column 2, with
the width of a column in blanks before it if column 1 is wanted; and equal is
column 3, with a column's width for each of the other two that is wanted.  A
line is written without the blanks and tabs at the front of it, and with the
newline that came with it.

THE DIFFERENCES

-wN could not have worked.  The number is read by btoi, handed 512 as the length
of the string, and the arguments are at the top of memory, so the end of the
string wraps and nothing is read; the width came out 0.  A width of 0 makes the
columns 0 wide, which is the "common" of the original here.  common.c reads the
number.  To test -wN against the original the sim was given a copy with the one
constant, at 04d2, changed from 512 to 13.

The standard input read twice, with the usage line, is as the original does, in
blocks of 512 - the library's buffer - for each.

A byte over 0177 is not written.  The characters go to putc as signed chars, and
putc of a negative number is a request to flush, so they are lost.  This is
kwic's, and common.c does the same.

The two line buffers are beside each other, and after them is the word that
holds the last comparison.  A line of 512 characters has no room for its end,
and the NUL goes over the first byte of the next thing: line2's first, for line1,
and the low byte of the comparison, for line2.  So a 512 character second line is
written with the byte of its own comparison after it, as it is - 'x' in the
case of a line against a different line of x's.  common.c has the same layout
and, in the cases tried, the same output.  It does not reproduce the original in
one that is only to be made by two files that are the same for 512 or more
characters in many lines at once - a file of 150 random lines, of which one in
eight is 512 or longer, against a copy of itself, differs in the byte that goes
after one of them.  The cause has not been found.

The exit status was whatever was left in BC; common.c exits 0, and 1 for a
file that will not open.

Checked in usersim against the original, standard error and all, with -wN on the
copy with the constant changed: sorted word lists with indented lines, in every
combination of -1, -2 and -3, with the options before the names and after, with
-w of 0, 5, 20, 30, 60 and 100 and with a -w that is not a number; files with
no newline at the last line, with bytes over 0177, with 512 character lines
against each other and against short ones, and 150 lines of mixed length against
a different file of the same kind; an empty first line; no arguments, one file,
three, and a file that will not open in either place.
